<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  What cppcheck is told to leave out when the gates run it over the
  generated C (scripts/check-runtime.sh).  Every entry has its reason
  above it and names as little as it can: a file where one file is
  meant, a symbol where one symbol is meant.  An entry without a
  reason is not added.  Each reason below was measured with a small case
  and a twin that differs in the one thing named (Cppcheck 2.17.1,
  2026-09-26; the entry for pctx: Cppcheck 2.21.0, 2026-10-05).  A
  symbol is named as the generated C names it: a parameter carries the
  prefix v_ there, so pzTail is v_pzTail (both gates went red on the
  old name, 2026-10-13).
-->
<suppressions>
  <!--
    cppcheck is not given the system headers, so a function a header
    declares is undeclared to it and it takes the function's result for an
    int: the bindings of SQLite, PCRE2, libtls and libcrypto return what
    the C functions return, which are pointers.  The C compiler reads the
    headers and refuses an int returned as a pointer (gcc 14 makes
    -Wint-conversion an error), so this check is kept by the compiler.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>CastIntegerToAddressAtReturn</id>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    Hilde.Pervasive's abort never returns, but Hilde has no way to say so:
    its C function carries no noreturn, and cppcheck follows the path
    past it.  In the ten programmes that divide by zero on purpose and in
    the one that negates the smallest Int32 it then reports the division
    and the negation that the guard before them stops.  The suites check
    that each of them ends with the guard's message.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>zerodiv</id>
    <fileName>*e2e_018-arithmetic-limits_*-div-zero.c</fileName>
  </suppress>
  <suppress>
    <id>integerOverflow</id>
    <fileName>*e2e_019-negation_005-int32-negate-min.c</fileName>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    The code generator writes a record as a compound literal with named
    members, (struct S){.bits = 2}, and cppcheck reads the member's
    initialiser as an assignment: two such literals in one call are
    reported as depending on the order of evaluation.  The same call with
    (struct S){2} is not reported.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>unknownEvaluationOrder</id>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    cppcheck reads an unsigned constant of 2^63 or more as negative:
    x >= 1 && x <= 18446744073709551614u on a uint64_t is reported as
    always false, and the same with 9223372036854775806u is not (x >= 5 &&
    x <= 1u is, as it should be).  The test of the boundaries of every
    width is where such a bound is written.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>incorrectLogicOperator</id>
    <fileName>*e2e_056-pattern-matching_011-boundaries-of-every-width.c</fileName>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    Standard.Sqlite passes a null pointer for the pzTail of
    sqlite3_prepare_v3 on purpose, and SQLite's documentation allows it:
    "If pzTail is not NULL then *pzTail is made to point to the first byte
    past the end of the first SQL statement".  cppcheck does not know that
    contract and reports the parameter.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>nullPointer</id>
    <symbolName>v_pzTail</symbolName>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    Standard.Libcrypto passes a null pointer for the pctx of
    EVP_DigestSignInit and EVP_DigestVerifyInit on purpose, and the manual
    of OpenSSL allows it: "If pctx is not NULL, the EVP_PKEY_CTX of the
    signing operation will be written to *pctx".  cppcheck does not know
    that contract and reports the parameter, as it does pzTail above.
    Cppcheck 2.21.0 (FreeBSD) reports it, five times in the generated C of
    three programmes; 2.17.1 (the Linux gate) does not.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>nullPointer</id>
    <symbolName>v_pctx</symbolName>
  </suppress>
  <!--
    A note about the analysis, not a finding in the code: at its normal
    check level cppcheck follows a limited number of branches of a
    function.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <id>normalCheckLevelMaxBranches</id>
  </suppress>
</suppressions>
